Network Protocols: The Rules of Communication

Network protocols are essential rules that ensure data is delivered correctly between devices on a network.
They define the order of delivery and the structure of data, allowing different devices to communicate effectively.
Key Protocols in Action

TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) establishes a connection and verifies devices before data streaming, often referred to as a "handshake."
ARP (Address Resolution Protocol) determines the MAC address of the next device in the data's path, ensuring it reaches the correct destination.
Accessing a Website: A Protocol Journey

HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure) provides a secure method for your browser to request and receive webpages from a server, encrypting data for security.
DNS (Domain Name System) translates human-readable domain names (like "yummyrecipesforme.org") into numerical IP addresses that computers use to locate websites.
